From 5e6134e19016fc985f3ccee2da6501d2587d88a7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: tomaszstaniak <68373859+tomaszstaniak@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 10 Nov 2026 17:46:57 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] fat: compare the write date with the cached entry, not a
 copy in the lock

Review of the previous commit: the date it last stored was kept at the
end of struct ExtFileLock. A lock adopted from another handler instance
in DoDiskInsert() is that instance's allocation; with an older binary,
writing the new fields went past its end.

OpWrite() now reads the file's entry after each write (it is in a cached
sector) and rewrites it when the size grew, the archive bit is missing
or the entry's write date is not the current FAT date. That still costs
at most one extra entry update per two seconds, a write after
SetFileDate() still updates the date, and a close without a write keeps
it. The fields in struct ExtFileLock and the loop in OpSetDate() are
gone; no handler structure changes.

OpWrite() set ATTR_ARCHIVE in the entry without updating gl->attr, so a
file without the archive bit had its entry rewritten on every write; the
global lock now follows the entry in OpWrite(), the ACTION_FINDOUTPUT
truncation and OpSetFileSize().

fatwritedate: the last step locks a file under one handler (FIRST
<path>, default the system's one), stops the volume and mounts it with
the HANDLER one, which adopts the lock. Examine() through the adopted
lock, SetFileDate() while it is held and UnLock() must work, and a later
write must date the file.
